    "case_id": "631",
    "case_summary": "A 31-year-old active duty man with no significant medical history presented with:- One week of worsening fatigue- Dyspnea- Hemoptysis- Nausea- Ataxia- Acute-onset headache\n\nInitial vital signs:- Blood pressure: 110/56 mm Hg- Heart rate: 104 beats/min- Respiratory rate: 29 breaths/min- Oxygen saturation: 83% to 87% on 6 L nasal cannulaPhysical findings:- Tachycardic- Shallow respirations- Diffuse crackles in the upper and lower lung fields\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Hemoglobin: 9.3 g/dL (showing anemia)- Platelets: 81,000 mcL (showing thrombocytopenia)- Lactate dehydrogenase: 2764 U/L (markedly elevated)Imaging Studies:1. Chest Radiograph:- Diffuse nodular pulmonary opacities- Right medial lung base opacities- Enlarged cardiac silhouette2. CT Chest, Abdomen, and Pelvis with contrast:- 2.0-cm right atrial mass- 7.8-cm left adrenal mass- 3.8-cm pelvic mass- Multiple pulmonary nodules- Pleural effusion3. Head CT (without contrast):- 3.4 cm subacute hemorrhage within right parietal lobe- Incidental pulmonary emboli noted4. Transthoracic Echocardiogram:- Left ventricle: small and underfilled, ejection fraction 60% to 65%- Right atrial mass with prolapse across tricuspid valve- Trace tricuspid and pulmonary valve regurgitation- Flattened septum with diastole5. Cardiac MRI:Title: Cardiac Mass ImagingDescription: 7.6 × 3.2 × 5.2–cm mass in the right atrium invading the right ventricle, superior vena cava, and inferior vena cava, with erosion into the pericardiumPathology:Core-needle biopsy of pelvic mass showed:- Malignant cells lining vascular spaces- Foci of necrosis, apoptosis, and occasional atypical mitoses- Immunoperoxidase staining: strongly positive for vimentin and CD31",
